# The EML tie-in (Odrzywolek 2026, arXiv:2603.21852). eml is an
# ordinary symbolic binary operator, established through its DEFINITION
#
#     eml(x, y) = exp(x) - ln(y)
#
# via the definitional extension provided by symbolic-core (rules SE),
# plus a small compiler into pure EML form.
#
# SIMPLIFICATION THROUGH DEFINITION: an eml term possesses neither a red
# form nor any rewrites intrinsic to itself. Its congruence rule states
# what it IS -- ((U eml V) expandsto ((exp of P) - (ln of Q))) using the
# normal forms P, Q of its components -- and symbolic-core simplifies
# that difference as an ordinary term. Every identity presented in the
# paper thereby emerges as a RESULT of the definition and the formal
# identities of exp, ln, and -, rather than as a rule:
#
#   eml(x, 1)                 = exp(x) - ln 1           -> (exp of x)
#   eml(1, 1)                 = exp(1) - ln 1           -> e
#   eml(1, eml(eml(1, z), 1)) = e - ln(exp(e - ln z))   -> (ln of z)   [Eq. (5)]
#
# Equation (5) applies x - (x - a) = a (symbolic-minus) in
# addition to exp/ln inversion; the zero
# eml(1, eml(eml(1, 1), 1)) takes e - e = 0
# (symbolic-minus's X - X). Normal forms exclude eml.
#
# The CONSTANT `e` is DEFINED within this context as exp(1): the
# rewrite (exp of &1) -> `e` shown below serves as its definition,
# rather than representing an identity. To the SIMPLIFIER, `e`
# functions as an opaque base constant; the compiler expands it,
# according to that definition, into eml(1, 1).
#
# COMPILER (expansion direction): (T emlcompile T) builds the pure-EML
# form in a bottom-up manner under the emlform relation; repeatable query
# ((T emlcompile T) = F). Coverage adheres to the macro chain of the
# paper's reference compiler (eml_compiler_v4.py; macros from SI
# Sect. 2.1, plus division x/y = x * (1/y), the compiler's eml_div, SI
# Sect. 2.5 item 7): exp, ln, -, +, neg, inv, *, /, eml itself, the
# constant e, and leaves. Composite operators DELEGATE to their defining
# term -- for instance, (U + V) materializes (U - (neg of V)) as a graph
# node, marks it, and harvests its emlform -- thereby transforming the
# reference compiler's recursion into standard fact propagation,
# resulting in output trees that are structurally identical to those
# generated by eml_compiler_v4.py's emit primitives.
# Two intentional departures from the reference PROGRAM: (1) the term is
# compiled AS WRITTEN, whereas the program compiles the canonical form
# that SymPy produces from its input. Thus, -, neg, inv, and / are
# compiled via the primitive of the operator itself, whereas the program
# emits larger trees (x - y: LeafCount 83, see eml.md). exp, ln, +, and *
# align only when SymPy preserves the term as written, but SymPy also
# reorders operands (y + x becomes x + y), combines identical components
# (x * x becomes x^2, x + x becomes 2 * x), and evaluates exp(ln x) to x.
# (2) Numerals stay opaque leaves (no eml_int double-and-add expansion,
# which would depend on representation). Declared constants other than e
# are treated as leaves, akin to SymPy Symbols. Output remains strictly
# pure EML whenever &1 is the sole numeral in the input. Unknown function
# symbols still yield NO emlform (partiality by absence).
#
# Identities are FORMAL (principal-branch and domain caveats as in
# symbolic-core.zph). In particular, the compiled negation template
# ln(1) - z goes through ln(ln 1) = ln 0, and exp(ln 0) = 0 holds as a
# formal identity.

.import symbolic-core
.import symbolic-minus

# Exclude the eml operator from the self-fact display sugar: facts
# like (&1 eml &1) are self-facts only because all terms are hash-consed
# -- coincidences of value, not request markers -- and must render verbose.
%(zelph/no-selffact-sugar "eml")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The constant e is defined as exp(1). This rewrite constitutes the
# DEFINITION of e; using it, eml(1, 1) = exp(1) - ln 1 simplifies to e in
# a single request.
#
# The equation ln e = 1 is the definition inverted, and it is needed:
# congruence turns (ln of (exp of &1)) into (ln of e) before the
# inverse-pair rule could recognize the exp, thus without this rule
# ln(exp(1)) would stop at ln e.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
e ~ symconst
(T red (exp of &1)) => (T rw e)
(T red (ln of e)) => (T rw &1)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Simplifier extension (1): decompose
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((U eml V) needssimp (U eml V)) => (U needssimp U)
((U eml V) needssimp (U eml V)) => (V needssimp V)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Simplifier extension (2): the definition. No red form, no rewrites --
# symbolic-core's rules SE simplify the right-hand side and hand its
# normal form back to the eml term.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((U eml V) needssimp (U eml V), U simp P, V simp Q)
=> ((U eml V) expandsto ((exp of P) - (ln of Q)))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiler: Trigger / Decompose / Base / Assemble / Connect
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T emlcompile T) => (T needseml T)

((F of U) needseml (F of U)) => (U needseml U)
((U eml V) needseml (U eml V)) => (U needseml U)
((U eml V) needseml (U eml V)) => (V needseml V)

# A declared atom functions as a leaf, just as a SymPy Symbol does in
# the reference compiler -- with the exception of the constant e: for
# the compiler, it is defined, not opaque, and compiles through its
# definition (see below, "Delegating operators").
(X needseml X, X ~ symvar) => (X emlform X)
(X needseml X, X ~ symconst, X != e) => (X emlform X)
((A cons R) needseml (A cons R)) => ((A cons R) emlform (A cons R))

((exp of U) needseml (exp of U), U emlform P)
=> ((exp of U) emlform (P eml &1))

((ln of U) needseml (ln of U), U emlform P)
=> ((ln of U) emlform (&1 eml ((&1 eml P) eml &1)))

((U eml V) needseml (U eml V), U emlform P, V emlform Q)
=> ((U eml V) emlform (P eml Q))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Macro chain (SI Sect. 2.1). Foundation: subtraction, assembled directly:
#   x - y  ->  eml(ln x, exp y)  =  eml(eml(1, eml(eml(1, x), 1)), eml(y, 1))
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((U - V) needseml (U - V)) => (U needseml U)
((U - V) needseml (U - V)) => (V needseml V)

((U - V) needseml (U - V), U emlform P, V emlform Q)
=> ((U - V) emlform ((&1 eml ((&1 eml P) eml &1)) eml (Q eml &1)))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Delegating operators: each seeds its defining term and harvests its
# emlform. The delegation DAG has no cycles (e -> {exp}, + -> {-, neg},
# neg -> {-, ln}, * -> {exp, +, ln}, / -> {*, inv}, inv -> {exp, neg, ln}),
# ensuring the cascade terminates; child markers are reached via the
# decompose rules of the auxiliary terms themselves.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The constant e = exp(1), as defined by the reference compiler's
# eml_const_E
(e needseml e) => ((exp of &1) needseml (exp of &1))

(e needseml e, (exp of &1) emlform F) => (e emlform F)

# Negation: -z = ln(1) - z
((neg of V) needseml (neg of V))
=> (((ln of &1) - V) needseml ((ln of &1) - V))

((neg of V) needseml (neg of V), ((ln of &1) - V) emlform F)
=> ((neg of V) emlform F)

# Addition: x + y = x - (-y)
((U + V) needseml (U + V))
=> ((U - (neg of V)) needseml (U - (neg of V)))

((U + V) needseml (U + V), (U - (neg of V)) emlform F)
=> ((U + V) emlform F)

# Reciprocal: 1/z = exp(-ln z)
((inv of V) needseml (inv of V))
=> ((exp of (neg of (ln of V))) needseml (exp of (neg of (ln of V))))

((inv of V) needseml (inv of V), (exp of (neg of (ln of V))) emlform F)
=> ((inv of V) emlform F)

# Multiplication: x * y = exp(ln x + ln y)
((U * V) needseml (U * V))
=> ((exp of ((ln of U) + (ln of V))) needseml (exp of ((ln of U) + (ln of V))))

((U * V) needseml (U * V), (exp of ((ln of U) + (ln of V))) emlform F)
=> ((U * V) emlform F)

# Division: x / y = x * (1/y)
((U / V) needseml (U / V))
=> ((U * (inv of V)) needseml (U * (inv of V)))

((U / V) needseml (U / V), (U * (inv of V)) emlform F)
=> ((U / V) emlform F)

(T emlcompile T, T emlform F) => ((T emlcompile T) = F)
